  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a deep trough remains over the East Coast as a \nridge stays centered over the Rocky Mountains. Temperatures will be \ncooler this morning with lows in the 60s for most of the Tennessee \nValley and in the 50s in Northeast Tennessee, Southwest Virginia and \nthe higher elevations. Some patchy fog will be possible mainly near \nbodies of water. Today and tomorrow will be dry with low humidity as \nhigh pressure remains over the Ohio Valley. Highs will be in the 80s \nfor most of the Tennessee Valley, sorry Chattanooga (90-92). Dew \npoints will be pleasant in the 50s at times. \n\nRain chances start to increase on Wednesday as a shortwave moves \ninto the Great Lakes Region. At the surface, a stationary boundary \nwill likely be over the Southeast U.S., bringing a chance for \nshowers and storms Wednesday evening and into the overnight hours. \nThursday afternoon will have the highest rain chances as a shortwave \nmoves through the Ohio Valley.\n\nFriday through the weekend looks mostly dry as the trough weakens. \nSome afternoon showers and storms will be possible mainly near the \nmountains and foothills.",
